<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ausonius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 310–c. 395</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Decimus Magnus Ausonius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Decimus Magnus Ausonius was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet and teacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a>, modern Bordeaux. After decades teaching rhetoric there, he became tutor to the future emperor Gratian, under whom he held high office and was consul in 379. His works include the Mosella, a poem on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, and poems on the professors of Bordeaux and his own family.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Decimus Magnus Ausonius was born about 310 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a>, the son of Julius Ausonius, a physician of Greek ancestry, and Aemilia Aeonia, from established land-owning Gallo-Roman families of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. His aunt and grandmother raised him strictly. He was educated at Bordeaux and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, where his maternal uncle Aemilius Magnus Arborius was a professor. He did well in grammar and rhetoric but said that his Greek was unsatisfactory. After training for a time as an advocate he preferred teaching, and in 334 he became a grammaticus at a school of rhetoric in Bordeaux, and afterwards a professor. His best-known pupil was the poet Paulinus, who later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Nola.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After thirty years of teaching, Ausonius was summoned by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> to teach his son Gratian, and accompanied them on the German campaigns of 368 and 369. Valentinian gave him the rank of quaestor, and at court in 369 he met Quintus Aurelius Symmachus. When Gratian became emperor in 375 he heaped honours on Ausonius and his family: Ausonius became Praetorian Prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, his father, nearly ninety, was given the rank of prefect of Illyricum, and in 376 his son Hesperius was made proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 379 Ausonius was awarded the consulate, the highest Roman honour.</p><p>In 383 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a> revolted and assassinated Gratian at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a>. Ausonius retired to his estates near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, which he called the nest of his old age, and spent the rest of his life composing poetry and writing to eminent contemporaries, many of them former pupils. He appears to have been a late convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. He died about 395.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known poems are the Mosella, a description of the River Moselle, and the Ephemeris, an account of a typical day, of which only the beginning and end survive. The Parentalia commemorates dead relatives, and the Commemoratio professorum Burdigalensium treats the teachers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> he had known. He also wrote epigrams, verse letters and a cento wedding poem built from lines of Vergil. His contemporaries admired him, but later critics rank him below the finest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> authors. Edward Gibbon wrote that his poetical fame condemns the taste of his age. His references to winemaking are cited as early evidence of large-scale viticulture around Bordeaux, and the Mosella mentions a water-driven saw cutting marble.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ausonius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Ausonius</b> was born there in the 4th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-vivisci">Bituriges Vivisci</a></li><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Ausonius</b> had family links with the town, and its district kept the name Bazadais.</blockquote><a href="/state/vasates">Vasates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bordeaux, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Langon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.84,-0.58&amp;year=365" aria-label="Bordeaux on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4536 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ausonius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4986 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.84,-0.58&amp;year=365">What was here in 365 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
